<title>HUNGARY: Hungary details economic forecast for 1997-2000.</title>
<headline>Hungary details economic forecast for 1997-2000.</headline>
<dateline>BUDAPEST 1997-05-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Hungary published on Wednesday the detailed figures of the government's economic forecast for the period between 1997 and 2000.</p>
<p>The figures released by the Finance Ministry are part of the three-year forecast approved by the government last week.</p>
<p>GROSS DOMESTIC PRODUCT (GDP) (in pct)</p>
<p>			   1996*   1997    1998    1999    2000</p>
<p> GDP		     0-1     2-3     3-4     3-4     4-5</p>
<p>- domestic usage</p>
<p>     of GDP	    -2	2-3     3-4     3-4     4-5</p>
<p>- household</p>
<p>     consumption   -4-(-3)    2	 2	 2	 3</p>
<p>- public</p>
<p>     consumption     -3	-4	 0	 0	 1</p>
<p>  - investments    -4-(-2)  11-13   12-14    12-14   10-12</p>
<p>* The Central Statistical Office's preliminary GDP growth figure for 1996 is one percent. The government said the GDP totalled 6,650 to 6,700 forints in 1996.</p>
<p>FOREIGN TRADE</p>
<p>Foreign trade</p>
<p>  balance ($ bln)    -2.7  -3-(-2.5) -3  -3.5-(-3) -3.5-(-3)</p>
<p> Export (pct change) 11-13   9-11   9-11     9-11     9-11</p>
<p> Import (pct change)  6-7    9-11   9-11     9-11     9-11</p>
<p>CURRENT ACCOUNT BALANCE (C/A) (in $ bln unless stated)</p>
<p>C/A balance</p>
<p>  (pct of GDP)	 -4    -4.5  -5-(-4.5) -5-(-4.5) -5-(-4.5)</p>
<p> C/A balance	   -1.7    -2  -2.5-(-2) -2.5(-2) -2.5</p>
<p>- net interest</p>
<p>     expenditure     -1.2    -1.1   -1.2     -1.2     -1.3</p>
<p>  - tourism balance   1.3     1.4  1.4-1.5    1.5	1.6</p>
<p>- unrequited</p>
<p>     transfers	  0.9     0.8    0.7	0.6     0.5-0.6</p>
<p>- cash direct</p>
<p>    investments	 1.8     1.5    1.5	1.5-2   1.5-2</p>
<p>- accumulated</p>
<p>  direct investments 13.3    14.5   15.5-16   17.5   19-19.5</p>
<p>CONSUMER PRICE INDEX (CPI) (in pct)</p>
<p>			   1996    1997    1998    1999    2000</p>
<p>			  (fact)</p>
<p> Annual average	+23.6   +18     +13.5    +10     +8</p>
<p> December yr/yr	+19.8   +17-18  +12	+9	+7</p>
<p>INDUSTRIAL PRODUCER PRICE INDEX (PPI) (in pct)</p>
<p> Average annual PPI  +22.6   +19-20  +13-14   +9-10   +8</p>
<p>ANNUAL AVERAGE FORINT DEVALUATION*</p>
<p>Annual devaluation</p>
<p>  rate (pct)	    18.7    15	11	 9	 7.5</p>
<p>Forint/dollar</p>
<p>exchange rate</p>
<p> (annual average)    152.7   184     205.5   219.5   229.5</p>
<p>* Hungary has a crawling peg forint devaluation system with a monthly depreciation rate of 1.1 percent.</p>
<p>FOREIGN DEBT ($ bln unless stated)</p>
<p> Gross foreign debt   27.6    28    28.5-29    29.5   30-30.5</p>
<p>Gross foreign debt</p>
<p>  (pct of GDP)	  63    63-64    62-63     60-6    57-58</p>
<p> Net foreign debt     14.3   14.5   15-15.5    16     16.5-17</p>
<p>Net foreign debt</p>
<p>  (pct of GDP)	  32-33   33     33-34	33     32</p>
<p>Debt service</p>
<p>  expenditure	    9.2     4.5    5	   5	5</p>
<p>Debt service</p>
<p>expenditure</p>
<p>  (pct of GDP)	  48	22-23  22-23     20-21   18</p>
<p>GOVERNMENT BUDGET BALANCE (pct of GDP)</p>
<p> Revenues*		48	45     41-42     40     38-39</p>
<p> Expenditures	   51	50     46-47     44     42</p>
<p> Balance*		 -3	-5**   -5   -4.5-(-4) -4-(-3.5)</p>
<p> Balance***	     4.5     3.5     2	1.5-2    1.5-2</p>
<p>* Without privatisation and one-off revenues.</p>
<p>** The rise of the deficit in 1997 is due to a technical accounting change as foreign debts have been transferred from the National Bank of Hungary to the central state budget.</p>
<p>*** Without debt service.</p>
<p>ANNUAL EARNINGS OF THE CORPORATE SECTOR (forint bln)*</p>
<p> Operating profit    1104    1417    1874     2269    2649</p>
<p>Pre-tax profit</p>
<p>  (balance)	     270     450     677	835    1025</p>
<p>  - profit		730     930    1157     1345    1545</p>
<p>  - loss		  460     480     480	510     520</p>
<p> Corporate tax	  102     111     154	178     204</p>
<p> Tax on dividend	  0	10	12	 15	18</p>
<p>*** Without banks.</p>
<p>-- Sandor Peto, Budapest newsroom (36 1) 327 4040</p>
